<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/1293">1293</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Dudan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Duden was a Mongol prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, sent by Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhta">Tokhta</a> to intervene in the quarrels of the Russian princes. In 1293 his army sacked Vladimir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> and more than a dozen other towns of north-eastern Rus' in support of Prince Andrey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorodets">Gorodets</a>. Russian chronicles remembered the campaign, known as Duden's raid, as one of the most destructive of Mongol rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/duden.jpg" alt="Duden" width="320" height="490" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Konrad Duden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duden_woerterbuch_iii.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
